eriments with a pretrained LLM L87:       1. cite77†Text interface. L88:       2. cite78†Training conditions. L89:       3. cite79†Optimization. L90:       4. cite80†Text evaluations. L91: cite81†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L92: \nL93: arXiv:2610.23051v1 [cs.AI] 16 Oct 2026\nL94: # Agentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction?\nL95: \nL96: Shiguang Wu Affiliation: Tsinghua University    Yaqing Wang Affiliation: Beijing Institute of Mathematical Sciences and Applications    Quanming Yao Affiliation: Tsinghua University\nL97: ###### Abstract\nL98: The learning algorithm view of in-context learning (ICL) studies how fixed-parameter Transformers learn from supplied examples. Language model agents extend this setting through interaction: their actions determine what evidence enters context next. We call this regime Agentic In-Context Learning (Agentic ICL). Existing studies largely depend on externally supplied contexts or reward-bearing interaction histories, while work on general tool-using agents lacks a learning algorithm view.\nL99: We represent an interactive learner by its state, update, and decision rule, covering active learning, bandits, and online reinforcement learning (RL). For expressivity, we construct causal Transformers that approximate these algorithms over a finite horizon, and bound the resulting loss in return. For training, we characterize when optimizing process rewards preserves behavior that maximizes the final outcome.\nL100: Distillation experiments show that Transformers can learn to execute three representative recursions. We then train models from outcome or process rewards without oracle action or state targets, as in RL post-training. Different rewards yield distinct decision policies. Improving the alignment of process supervision with outcome-optimal action preferences shifts learned decisions accordingly. Such behavioral differences persist in a pretrained language model across textual paraphrases.\nL101: ## 1 Introduction\nL102: In-context learning (ICL) is a central way in which language models adapt at inference time: information in the prompt changes subsequent predictions while the model parameters remain fixed.\nL103: This has motivated a learning algorithm view of ICL, which asks what estimator, update rule, or other learning computation a Transformer (cite82†Vaswani et al., 2017 ) implements through its forward passes over an externally supplied prompt (cite83†Xie et al., 2022 ; cite84†Garg et al., 2022 ; cite85†Akyürek et al., 2023 ; cite86†von Oswald et al., 2023 ; cite87†Bai et al., 2023 ; cite88†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL104: Modern language-model agents routinely operate in an analogous but closed-loop regime. An agent can browse the web, call tools, edit code, or run experiments, then use the returned results to choose later actions (cite89†Yao et al., 2023 ; cite90†Shinn et al., 2023 ; cite91†Zhao et al., 2024 ; cite92†Lutz et al., 2024 ). A search query determines which page is read, a code edit determines the next test result, and an experiment determines the next measurement.\nL105: The agent therefore does not merely learn from a supplied context: through its actions, it helps determine the context from which it will learn next.\nL106: We call this execution regime Agentic In-Context Learning (Agentic ICL). Starting from an initial prompt or task description, a fixed-parameter model chooses an action, receives feedback, and incorporates that feedback into its next decision. Its context grows through interaction with the environment. The returned feedback can be a search result, compiler error, measurement, critique, or reward.\nL107: This motivates extending the learning algorithm view of ICL to interactive computations in which decisions shape subsequent evidence. The central question is:\nL108: > What interactive algorithms can Transformers learn in context, and how do training objectives shape their behavior?\nL109: In-context reinforcement learning (ICRL) provides the closest precedent (cite93†Laskin et al., 2023 ; cite94†Lee et al., 2023 ; cite95†Lin et al., 2024 ; cite96†Wang et al., 2025 ; cite97†Liang and Lai, 2026 ), but requires scalar rewards to be included in the interaction history. However, an agent usually receives non-reward feedback: a search result, compiler diagnostic, or measurement can guide the next action. We therefore develop Agentic ICL allowing agents to learn from non-reward feedback.\nL110: This also separates the evidence used for in-context adaptation from the objective used for training and evaluation. Agentic ICL includes ICRL as a special case. Figure cite98†1 illustrates this relationship.\nL111: Figure 1: Illustrative comparison of Agentic ICL, ICL and ICRL. Agentic ICL studies how agents solve general agentic tasks.\nL112: \nL113: Task performance alone does not reveal the interactive algorithm. Answering these questions requires both a construction showing what Transformers can execute and experiments examining what they learn under different objectives. Our contributions are fourfold:\nL114: \nL115:   * •\nL116: Closed-loop framework (Section cite13†3 ). We factor an interactive learner into a sufficient state, a feedback update, and a decision rule. The same interface covers active learning, bandits, and online RL.\nL117: \nL118:   * •\nL119: \nL120: Transformer realization (Section cite16†4 ). We give a constructive proof that compiles regular finite-horizon recursions into causal Transformers. The construction realizes their action choices and yields a bound on autonomous return loss.\nL121: \nL122:   * •\nL123: Training objectives and diagnosis (Section cite17†5 ). We theoretically characterize when process supervision preserves outcome-optimal behavior, then develop behavioral diagnostics for trained Transformers.\nL124: \nL125:   * •\nL126: \nL127: What training learns (Section cite20†6 ). Supervised distillation tests learned execution of supplied interactive recursions. RL without oracle action shows how outcome and process rewards lead to different decisions, and how those decisions change with controllable reward intervention.\nL128: ## 2 Related Work\nL129: #### Learning algorithm views of ICL and ICRL.\nL130: ICL has been characterized as implicit Bayesian inference, regression, gradient descent, algorithm selection, and data-dependent learning-to-learn (cite83†Xie et al., 2022 ; cite84†Garg et al., 2022 ; cite85†Akyürek et al., 2023 ; cite86†von Oswald et al., 2023 ; cite87†Bai et al., 2023 ; cite88†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL131: Constructive, behavioral, and mechanistic studies identify familiar estimators inside Transformers (cite85†Akyürek et al., 2023 ; cite86†von Oswald et al., 2023 ; cite87†Bai et al., 2023 ); we extend this standard to closed-loop recursions whose decisions determine subsequent in-context data.\nL132: ICRL extends this perspective to RL updates and control, including pretraining on stored state-action-reward trajectories with source-policy or oracle actions (cite93†Laskin et al., 2023 ; cite94†Lee et al., 2023 ; cite95†Lin et al., 2024 ). These models can nevertheless generate their own interaction histories at deployment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNot All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41223view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"lineno\":85}); Total lines: 472\nL76: Test-time scaling is particularly effective when additional computation contributes evidence that is sufficiently complementary to the model’s current belief, allowing errors to be corrected rather than repeatedly reinforced. However, computation from the same model can remain shaped by persistent model-specific error bias, limiting the diversity of the evidence it provides. We observe three manifestations of this phenomenon across generation, reasoning, and self-evaluation (Figure cite64†1 ).\nL77: cite65†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Three manifestations of model-specific error bias within a single reasoning model. (a) When repeated sampling produces an incorrect majority answer, erroneous samples concentrate on the same wrong answer rather than spreading across alternatives. (b) When a model’s initial answer is incorrect, reasoning frequently preserves the same wrong answer rather than correcting it.\nL78: (c) When answers at different reasoning stages disagree, the model’s self-evaluation systematically favors the later, more elaborate outcome, even when the earlier answer is correct. Detailed analyses are in Section cite7†2 .\nL79: (a) Generation bias under repeated sampling. Repeated sampling can diversify reasoning traces without necessarily diversifying their failures. When self-consistency produces an incorrect majority answer, erroneous samples frequently concentrate on the same dominant wrong answer rather than spreading across alternatives, indicating a persistent generation bias rather than independent error modes (Section cite8†2.1 ).\nL80: (b) Initial-answer persistence bias. Reasoning does not necessarily provide evidence independent of a model’s initial judgment. When a model is first asked to answer directly and then asked to reason before answering, its post-reasoning prediction can remain strongly associated with the initial answer; in particular, an initially incorrect prediction may persist through subsequent reasoning rather than being corrected (Section cite9†2.2 ).\nL81: Prior work similarly suggests that reasoning models can form strong preliminary answer preferences (cite66†Dang et al., 2026 ), that extended reasoning can lead to overthinking (cite67†Zhou et al., 2026 ), and that early errors may propagate through subsequent reasoning (cite68†Zhang et al., 2024 ).\nL82: (c) Longer-reasoning preference bias. A related bias appears when the model is asked to evaluate competing answers produced at different stages of its own reasoning. Rather than judging the candidates symmetrically, the model strongly favors the later, more elaborate reasoning outcome, even when the earlier answer is correct.\nL83: In our experiments, the model selects the later-stage answer in over 95% of disagreements and remains highly confident even when this preference leads to an incorrect decision (Section cite10†2.3 ). This behavior is related to previously documented length and verbosity biases in LLM-based evaluation (cite69†Zheng et al., 2023 ; cite70†Hu et al., 2025 ).\nL84: Together, these observations suggest that:\nL85: \nL86: model-specific error bias persists across generation, reasoning, and self-evaluation, that is, the three stages of same-model computation that test-time scaling methods rely on. More compute from the same model is therefore not more evidence.\nL87: \nL88: These findings suggest that useful additional computation should provide evidence that is both selectively acquired and complementary to the base model’s existing reasoning.\nL89: (1) When to seek additional evidence. Prior work shows that intermediate predictions and answer-stability signals can reveal how reasoning evolves and support adaptive computation (cite56†Yang et al., 2026 ; cite57†Fu et al., 2025 ; cite58†Dai et al., 2025 ), while extended reasoning is not always beneficial (cite67†Zhou et al., 2026 ). Motivated by these observations, we use disagreement between an intermediate prediction and the final prediction as a simple per-question signal of reasoning instability.\nL90: (2) Where the evidence should come from. For such cases, we seek complementary evidence from heterogeneous LLMs. Prior ensemble studies show that different models exhibit complementary strengths and can succeed on different subsets of problems (cite71†Jiang et al., 2023 ; cite72†Wang et al., 2025 ), suggesting that model heterogeneity can reduce dependence on the particular error tendencies of the base model.\nL91: (3) How the evidence should be combined. We let these solvers reason independently, without seeing the base model’s candidate predictions, and aggregate their answers directly, consistent with evidence that voting-based ensembles can be competitive with more elaborate multi-agent protocols (cite61†Li et al., 2024 ; cite73†Smit et al., 2024 ).\nL92: These three principles lead to Dia (D isagreement-triggered I ndependent A rbitration): agreement between the intermediate and final predictions is returned directly, whereas such reasoning-stage disagreement activates a small heterogeneous solver panel for candidate-blind, answer-level arbitration.\nL93: Across seven benchmarks and two base models (Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B; cite74†Yang et al., 2025 ), Dia outperforms full chain-of-thought in 12 of 14 settings, including an improvement from 59.0% to 66.0% on MuSiQue (cite75†Trivedi et al., 2022 ), while activating on only ${\\sim}$17% of examples and using up to 47.2% fewer tokens than a fully-connected multi-agent baseline that invokes every agent on all questions.\nL94: A multi-agent baseline that routes independent agent outputs through a centralized judge underperforms direct answer aggregation, e.g., 60.67% vs. 66.00% on MuSiQue with Qwen3-4B, highlighting the importance of the aggregation protocol.\nL95: Contributions.\nL96: \nL97:   1. ①\nL98: \nL99: Three manifestations of model-specific error bias. We identify systematic error persistence across three stages of same-model computation: repeated sampling can reproduce a shared wrong answer, reasoning can preserve an initially incorrect prediction, and self-evaluation can systematically favor a later and more elaborate reasoning outcome (Section cite7†2 ).\nL100: \nL101:   2. ②\nL102: Disagreement-triggered independent arbitration. To mitigate model-specific error bias, we propose Dia, a framework that detects reasoning instability via reasoning-stage disagreement and resolves it through candidate-blind, answer-level arbitration by heterogeneous external solvers (Section cite11†3 ).\nL103: \nL104:   3. ③\nL105: Comprehensive evaluation. Across seven benchmarks and two base models, Dia improves over full chain-of-thought in 12 of 14 settings while activating on only ${\\sim}$17% of examples and using up to 47.2% fewer tokens than the multi-agent baseline (Section cite18†4 ).\nL106: ## 2 Motivation\nL107: ### 2.1 Generation Bias under Repeated Sampling\nL108: \nL109: Repeated sampling from a reasoning model can produce varied reasoning traces while sharing the same failure pattern. We quantify this effect on questions where majority-vote self-consistency produces an incorrect answer. For each failed question $q$, we denote by $C_{q}$ the fraction of incorrect samples assigned to the most frequent wrong answer.\nL110: \nL111: cite76†Image: Refer to caption L112: Dataset  | Initially Wrong  | Error Retained\nL113: Qwen3-8B\nL114: ---\nL115: MMLU-Pro  | 52.29%  | 26.23%\nL116: MuSR  | 42.00%  | 61.90%\nL117: DetectBench  | 43.53%  | 35.54%\nL118: Qwen3-Next-80B- A3B-Instruct\nL119: ---\nL120: MMLU-Pro  | 41.43%  | 32.41%\nL121: MuSR  | 37.50%  | 58.67%\nL122: DetectBench  | 39.07%  | 34.86%\nL123: Figure 2: Generation bias and initial-answer persistence bias. Left: Wrong-answer concentration under SC@20 failures. Each point denotes a failed question and reports its dominant wrong-answer share $C_{q}$; dashed lines mark the uniform-choice baseline. Right: Initial-answer persistence across base-model scales. Error Retained denotes the fraction of initially incorrect answers for which full CoT returns the same incorrect answer.\nL124: Figure cite77†2 shows the distribution of $C_{q}$ under SC@20 on MMLU-Pro and GPQA-Diamond. Across models and datasets, the wrong answers are more concentrated than the uniform-choice baseline. For many failed questions, all incorrect samples converge on a single answer ($C_{q}=1$).\nL125: Figure cite77†2 shows three patterns. First, when repeated samples from the same model are wrong, they often collapse onto a single answer rather than spread across alternatives. Second, error concentration increases with model scale. Third, concentration is higher in the reasoning-oriented models. Takeaway. Increasing the sampling budget of the same model may repeatedly reinforce an existing error mode rather than introduce independent evidence.\nL126: ### 2.2 Initial-Answer Persistence Bias\nL127: Reasoning is expected to provide additional evidence beyond a model’s initial judgment. However, a model may already exhibit a strong answer preference before any explicit reasoning, and the same preference can remain after full reasoning. To examine this initial-answer persistence, we first ask the model to produce a direct answer without reasoning and ask the same model to solve the same question with full chain-of-thought reasoning.\nL128: We measure whether the final answer retains the initial answer, particularly when the initial answer is incorrect.\nL129: The right panel of Figure cite77†2 shows that full CoT often reproduces the model’s initially incorrect answer rather than independently reassessing the question. Same-error persistence is strongest on MuSR and remains visible across model scales and datasets. Takeaway. Additional reasoning can elaborate an existing error without supplying the independent evidence needed to overturn it.\nL130: ### 2.3 Longer-Reasoning Preference Bias\nL131: A model’s prediction can change as reasoning proceeds. To capture this change, we compare an intermediate-stage prediction, obtained at an intermediate reasoning stage where the model is sufficiently confident, with the final-stage prediction produced after the model completes its reasoning. When the two disagree, which we call a reasoning-stage disagreement, one simple way to resolve the conflict is to let the model select between its own two predictions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCausal Edit Serialization (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1)\nciteturn41223view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 1228\nL28:       1. cite18†Applying edits and continuing generation. L29:     7. cite19†3.7 GEC Post-Training L30:   5. cite20†4 Results L31:     1. cite21†4.1 Repair Task and Baselines L32:     2. cite22†4.2 Experimental Settings L33:     3. cite23†4.3 Pretraining Comparison and Representation Cost L34:       1. cite24†Language modeling and general capabilities (A/B). L35:       2. cite25†Complete repair (C/D). L36:       3. cite26†Partial repair (E/F). L37:       4. cite27†Localization and payload generation. L38:       5. cite28†Pretrained adaptation. L39:       6. cite29†Representation cost and preservation (G). L40:     4. cite30†4.4 Post-Training: Judged Grammatical Correction L41:       1. cite31†Evaluation and decoding policy. L42:       2. cite32†Unfiltered edits and selectivity. L43:       3. cite33†Conservative acceptance policy. L44:     5. cite34†4.5 Continuation After Edit Programs L45:   6. cite35†5 Conclusion L46:   7. cite36†6 Limitations and Future Work L47:     1. cite37†Coverage and generalization. L48:     2. cite38†Post-training and measurement. L49:     3. cite39†Systems and next steps. L50:     4. cite40†Future work. L51:   8. cite41†Funding. L52:   9. cite42†Competing interests. L53:   10. cite43†References L54:   11. cite44†A Reproducibility, Protocols, and Asset Provenance L55:     1. cite45†A.1 Training budgets and implementation L56:       1. cite46†Shared causal pretraining settings. L57:       2. cite47†Iterative training. L58:       3. cite48†Supervised CoEdIT fine-tuning. L59:       4. cite49†Pretrained-decoder adaptation. L60:     2. cite50†A.2 Metric and evaluation contracts L61:       1. cite51†Clean-language evaluation. L62:       2. cite52†Editing metrics. L63:     3. cite53†A.3 Compute resources L64:   12. cite54†B Corruption Distribution and Data Quality L65:     1. cite55†Edit arrival and lengths. L66:     2. cite56†Token-budgeted serialization. L67:     3. cite57†Insert-content source mixture. L68:     4. cite58†Protecting a prefix as stable context. L69:     5. cite59†Source text and generated edits. L70:     6. cite60†Copy mining and source differences. L71:     7. cite61†B.1 Structural and semantic quality L72:   13. cite62†C Edit Decoding and Cache Management L73:     1. cite63†Terminal editing. L74:     2. cite64†Continuation from the serialized history. L75:     3. cite65†Continuation from materialized text. L76:   14. cite66†D Supervision Ablations L77:     1. cite67†Position-loss weight sweep. L78:     2. cite68†Training-data source sweep. L79:     3. cite69†Corruption-exposure sweep. L80:   15. cite70†E Decoding-policy sensitivity L81:   16. cite71†F Baseline Architectures and Edit Interfaces L82:     1. cite72†F.1 Shared conventions L83:     2. cite73†F.2 CES and pretrained-decoder adaptation L84:     3. cite74†F.3 AR-DSL: numeric addresses in ordinary text L85:     4. cite75†F.4 Flat-relative: addresses as backward-distance tokens L86:     5. cite76†F.5 Whole-target regeneration L87:     6. cite77†F.6 Oracle-span fill-in-the-middle L88:     7. cite78†F.7 Predicted-span fill-in-the-middle L89:     8. cite79†F.8 Iterative Levenshtein editing L90:     9. cite80†F.9 Clean autoregressive reference L91:       1. cite81†Comparison scope. L92:   17. cite82†G Extended baseline and human-transfer results L93:     1. cite83†G.1 CES with supplied edit locations L94:   18. cite84†H Autoregressive continuation after an edit program L95:     1. cite85†H.1 Held-out natural-text likelihood L96:   19. cite86†I Representation, Editing, and Continuation Costs L97:     1. cite87†I.1 Representation and Retained-Context Costs L98:     2. cite88†I.2 Measured Editing Latency L99:     3. cite89†I.3 Cache Rebuilding and Resumed Generation L100:   20. cite90†J Post-Training Recipe and Evaluation L101:     1. cite91†Supervision and pointer anchor. L102:     2. cite92†RL data and rollout settings. L103:     3. cite93†Frozen evaluation partitions. L104:     4. cite94†Verifiable reward and policy objective. L105:     5. cite95†Target-blind inference gate. L106:     6. cite96†Full confirmation acceptance policy. L107:     7. cite97†J.1 Confirmation judging and sensitivity L108:     8. cite98†J.2 Unfiltered decoding and threshold sweep L109:   21. cite99†K Shared Pointer Tokens and Further Directions L110: cite100†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L111: \nL112: arXiv:2610.22820v1 [cs.CL] 16 Oct 2026\nL113: # Causal Edit Serialization\nL114: \nL115: Louis Robinson Affiliation: Independent Researcher Email: louis97rf@gmail.com\nL116: ###### Abstract\nL117: Standard autoregressive language models generate text sequentially, but revision requires referring back to tokens in the input context or those already generated. Causal Edit Serialization (CES) is introduced as a framework for expressing such revisions within the same causal token stream. Special pointer tokens are paired with predicted source addresses, allowing insertion, deletion, replacement, copying and movement to be localized explicitly.\nL118: A lightweight localization head and dual-index rotary position encoding support both address prediction and conditioning on previously emitted pointers, while retaining a standard causal attention mask. A pretraining procedure combines ordinary language modeling with serialized repairs derived from random corruptions, model-generated edits and repeated text.\nL119: Across model scales and editing baselines, CES models demonstrate complete applied edits with modestly higher clean-text bits per byte than clean autoregressive models. Supervised and reinforcement-learning post-training provide a grammatical-error-correction proof of concept, producing more beneficial than harmful changes under reference-blind model judging. Correct repair programs also improve subsequent text prediction while the original context and its cached states are retained.\nL120: CES provides a common interface for generation and localized revision, with potential extensions to iterative refinement and additional pointer-addressed transformations.\nL121: ## 1 Introduction\nL122: Editing is fundamental in natural language and code: humans revise spans, replace phrases, move clauses, duplicate content, and repair earlier generations (cite101†Mallinson et al., 2020 ; cite102†Reid and Neubig, 2022 ). Standard autoregressive language models, however, are optimized to emit the next token from left to right (cite103†Radford et al., 2019 ; cite104†Brown et al., 2020 ).\nL123: Regenerating a revised passage preserves causal decoding, but re-emits unchanged text, spending output tokens and decoding work on content already available. Emitting a localized edit avoids this repetition. The challenge is to identify the affected source spans and make those references available to subsequent predictions.\nL124: Denoising encoder–decoder models reconstruct text from corrupted inputs (cite105†Raffel et al., 2023 ; cite106†Song et al., 2019 ). FELIX combines tagging and insertion (cite101†Mallinson et al., 2020 ), while insertion-based and iterative editing models relax left-to-right output construction (cite107†Stern et al., 2019 ; cite108†Patel et al., 2025 ; cite109†Gu et al., 2019 ). Here, explicit edit programs are generated within a single causal token stream.\nL125: Source locations are predicted directly, and subsequent decisions condition on those references, while the standard causal attention mask is retained. Replacement, copy, and move should be available alongside insertion and deletion.\nL126: This paper presents Causal Edit Serialization (CES). CES appends a structured edit stream after the base sequence and factors each edit into a short causal chain: predict an operation token, localize the relevant source span or destination, and then emit any payload tokens. A dual-index rotary scheme lets the model reason both about global token order and about edit addresses in the source sequence, while a lightweight localization head predicts source positions directly from the hidden states.\nL127: The formulation supports insert, delete, replace, move, and copy, all inside the same decoder-only model.\nL128: The contributions are:\nL129: \nL130:   1. 1.\nL131: \nL132: A representation and training procedure for causal editing. CES combines source-address pointers and dual-index positional encoding with training on random corruptions, model-generated edits, and mined copy examples.\nL133: \nL134:   2. 2.\nL135: A comparison of pretrained editing models. Three depths and three training seeds compare CES with autoregressive edit scripts using numeric addresses (AR-DSL, an autoregressive domain-specific language) or relative-distance tokens, full-text regeneration, infilling with given or predicted spans, iterative insertion/deletion, and adaptation of a clean decoder.\nL136: \nL137:   3. 3.\nL138: Beneficial grammatical correction after post-training. Supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning produce positive net improvement on grammatical error correction (GEC) under reference-blind GPT-5 Mini judging.\nL139: \nL140:   4. 4.\nL141: \nL142: Continuation conditioned on edit programs. Supplied correct repairs improve subsequent clean-text likelihood while the source and edit program remain in the causal history.\nL143: ## 2 Related Work\nL144: #### Insertion and iterative editing.\nL145: The Insertion Transformer (cite107†Stern et al., 2019 ), Insertion Language Models (cite108†Patel et al., 2025 ), and Levenshtein Transformer (cite109†Gu et al., 2019 ) support generation orders or refinement operations beyond appending text. FELIX (cite101†Mallinson et al., 2020 ) combines tagging and insertion, while cite102†Reid and Neubig (2022) model editing processes. EditNTS (cite110†Dong et al., 2019 ) autoregressively generates edit operations with a cursor that advances through the source.\nL146: CES makes source addresses explicit within the generated edit stream. The contribution of CES is the integration of explicit source addressing with causal edit generation.\nL147: #### Infilling, denoising, and patch interfaces.\nL148: T5 (cite105†Raffel et al., 2023 ) and MASS (cite106†Song et al., 2019 ) use reconstruction objectives with encoder–decoder architectures. Fill-in-the-middle training can also use a standard causal decoder by rearranging prefix, suffix, and middle (cite111†Bavarian et al., 2022 ); causal attention alone therefore does not distinguish CES from FIM. The distinction is predicting source locations and heterogeneous operations together, rather than receiving a hole to fill.\n"
